{"id":"73209c02-307c-42be-aeb2-d7025b81547f","object":"chat.completion","created":1784992398,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        pid = n['parent']\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None][0]\\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_by_id.keys(), key=lambda x: (-node_by_id[x]['depth'], x))\\n    removed = []\\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\\n        if not node['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbols_in_retained = {node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained}\\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_retained):\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n        else:\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-01. Looking at the fixture payloads, we have a tree with nodes having id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. There is required_symbols list. The task: grammar-guided node reduction, preserving interestingness oracle (probably meaning required symbols must remain?), and ensure validity.\n\nThe output should have removed_node_ids (list of strings) and retained_node_ids (list of strings). We need to iteratively remove nodes that are removable, but only if they are not root, not required symbol, and not with retained children? The description: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" Also: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" So we need to simulate a reduction process: consider nodes in some order (deeper first, then by ID as tiebreak). For each node that is removable, check if removing it would still preserve all required symbols (i.e., every required symbol appears in at least one retained node after removal). Also ensure node has no retained children (if we are removing a node, its children must also be removed? Actually \"a node with retained children\" means we cannot remove a node if any of its descendants are retained. So we should only remove leaf nodes (or nodes whose entire subtree is removed). So essentially we traverse from leaves upward, removing removable nodes if condition holds.\n\nAlso \"Do not mutate the input.\" So we work on a copy of nodes list or build sets.\n\nWe need to return removed_node_ids (order of removal?) and retained_node_ids (sorted? says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids should be sorted, removed_node_ids should be in the order they were removed.\n\nLet's parse the case payload: nodes list, required_symbols list. We'll build a dictionary mapping id to node, also children mapping.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n- Build dict node_by_id\n- Build parent mapping (id -> parent id), and children mapping (id -> list of child ids)\n- Identify root (parent null)\n- Build set of required symbols from required_symbols list.\n- Identify initial retained set: all nodes (since none removed yet)\n- Create list of nodes sorted by depth descending (deeper first), and for equal depth, by id ascending (since \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\"). Actually \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So sort by -depth, then id.\n- Initialize removed list.\n- Iterate over that sorted list of node ids (candidates for removal). For each:\n  - Check if node is removable (node['removable'] == True)\n  - Check if node is root? root id is 'root'? Actually root has id \"root\" and parent null. So if node['id'] == root_id? But root is not removable anyway? In fixtures, root has removable=false. So skip if not removable.\n  - Check if node has any retained children: if any child id is still in retained set (not removed). If so, skip.\n  - Check if removing this node would cause any required symbol to become missing. That is, after removal, is every required symbol still present in some retained node? But note: nodes may have symbols that are not required; required symbols must be present. We need to ensure that after removing this node, all required symbols are still represented among retained nodes. This involves checking if this node's symbol is required and if there are other nodes with that symbol. If it is the only node with that required symbol, then cannot remove.\n  - Also \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" Already handled.\n  - If all conditions satisfied, remove node: add id to removed list, remove from retained set.\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted list of remaining ids.\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nBut we need to consider that removal of a node might make its parent become a leaf candidate for later removal. Since we traverse deeper first, when we attempt to remove a node, its children are already considered for removal earlier (since deeper nodes come first). So if we remove a child node, parent later may become leaf. That's fine.\n\nEdge cases: nodes might have same symbol; required symbols might be satisfied by multiple nodes.\n\nAlso note: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" Should we also ensure that after removal, the tree is still \"grammar valid\"? For this simplified task, perhaps the only grammar constraint is that required symbols remain. The description says \"grammar-valid deletion\" but no explicit grammar rules given. Probably the required_symbols condition is the interestingness oracle. So I'll implement as above.\n\nLet's test with the first fixture. We have nodes:\n\nroot (depth0, removable false, symbol root) - required\nn1 (depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s1) - required, not removable\nn2 (depth1, parent root, removable true, symbol s2) - not required\nn3 (depth1, parent root, removable false, symbol s0) - not required\nn4 (depth2, parent n2, removable true, symbol s1) - required\nn5 (depth2, parent n3, removable false, symbol s2) - not required\nn6 (depth2, parent n1, removable true, symbol s0) - not required\n\nRequired symbols: root, s1\n\nSorted by depth desc: depth2 nodes first: n4, n5, n6; then depth1: n1, n2, n3; then root. Within same depth, sort by id (lexicographic). So order: n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root.\n\nStart retained set all ids.\n\nCheck n4: removable true, check retained children: none (leaf). Check required symbols: n4 symbol is s1, which is required. Are there other nodes with s1? Yes, n1 also has s1. So removing n4 still leaves n1 with s1. So remove n4. removed=[n4], retained set minus n4.\n\nCheck n5: removable false, skip.\n\nCheck n6: removable true, leaf. Symbol s0 not required. No retained children. Remove? Required symbols: root and s1 are still present (root, n1, n2, n3, n5). So yes. Remove n6. removed=[n4,n6].\n\nCheck n1: removable false, skip.\n\nCheck n2: removable true. Has children? n4 was removed, so no retained children. Symbol s2 not required. Remove? Required symbols: root (root), s1 (n1 still). Yes remove. removed=[n4,n6,n2].\n\nCheck n3: removable false, skip.\n\nCheck root: removable false, skip.\n\nRetained ids: root, n1, n3, n5? Actually n5 is still there (removable false). So retained set: root, n1, n3, n5. Sorted: ['n1','n3','n5','root'].\n\nThus expected output: removed_node_ids: ['n4','n6','n2'], retained_node_ids: ['n1','n3','n5','root'].\n\nBut is that correct? Let's verify if n3 (depth1, parent root, symbol s0) should be retained? It is not required, but it is not removable, so it stays. Similarly n5 (depth2, parent n3, symbol s2, not removable) stays. That seems plausible.\n\nBut the task says \"Perses syntax-guided reduction on a miniature grammar\". In Perses, reduction proceeds by trying to delete nodes and checking if the resulting string still parses and passes interestingness test. Here we have no actual grammar or string. The required_symbols likely simulate interestingness. So I think above approach is correct.\n\nWe must also handle that if a node is removable but its symbol is required and it's the only one with that symbol, we cannot remove it. That would be the case if e.g. n1 were removable. But it's not.\n\nNow implement solve(case) in Python. We'll import json? The function must return a dict/list that is JSON-serializable, but we don't need to serialize to string; the evaluator expects a Python object that can be serialized. We can just return dict.\n\nImportant: The case payload is a dict with keys 'nodes' and 'required_symbols'. We must not mutate.\n\nLet's write code.\n\nWe'll define solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = case['required_symbols']\n    # Build maps\n    node_by_id = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = {n['id']: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        pid = n['parent']\n        if pid is not None:\n            children[pid].append(n['id'])\n    root_id = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None][0]  # assume one root\n    # Initial retained set\n    retained = set(node_by_id.keys())\n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_ids = sorted(node_by_id.keys(), key=lambda x: (-node_by_id[x]['depth'], x))\n    removed = []\n    for nid in sorted_ids:\n        node = node_by_id[nid]\n        if not node['removable']:\n            continue\n        # Skip root (though root shouldn't be removable)\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        # Check if has retained children\n        has_children = any(c in retained for c in children[nid])\n        if has_children:\n            continue\n        # Check required symbols remain after removal\n        # Temporarily remove from retained set for checking\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        # Now check if all required symbols are present in remaining retained nodes\n        symbols_in_retained = set(node_by_id[rid]['symbol'] for rid in retained)\n        if required_symbols.issubset(symbols_in_retained):\n            # Removal is valid, keep removed\n            removed.append(nid)\n            # retained already updated\n        else:\n            # Cannot remove, restore retained\n            retained.add(nid)\n    # After loop, retained set is final retained ids\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    # Return\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n\nCheck: required_symbols is a list, we convert to set for subset check.\n\nEdge: required_symbols is given as list, maybe with duplicates? We'll treat as set.\n\nAlso note: The root might have required symbol \"root\", but it's not removable anyway.\n\nTest with first fixture quickly in mind.\n\nI think it's correct.\n\nNow we need to output the implementation as a JSON string in the required envelope. The entire response must be exactly one valid JSON object with key \"implementation\", value being the Python source as a string (JSON-escaped). No markdown, no extra text.\n\nSo we'll produce: {\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"}\n\nWe need to escape newlines and backslashes etc. In Python, we can use json.dumps to create the JSON string for the implementation, then embed it.\n\nExample: implementation_source = \"def solve(case):\\n    ...\"\nThen output = json.dumps({\"implementation\": implementation_source})\n\nBut we need to ensure the entire response is that JSON object, no extra characters.\n\nI'll write the code and then produce the final answer.\n\nOne more nuance: The output schema says retained_node_ids must be an array of strings, and removed_node_ids array of strings, both required. Our code returns that.\n\nAlso ensure we use only standard library. We don't need to import anything besides maybe json for debugging, but we don't need to import json for the function itself because we just return dict. So no imports needed.\n\nPotential issue: The sorted_ids order: we use tuple (-depth, id). But id is string. That's okay.\n\nAlso, root removal condition: we check if nid == root_id, but also if node['removable'] is false, so root won't be removed anyway. But we can keep the check for safety.\n\nLet's write the implementation."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":2967,"total_tokens":5136,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2633},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}